WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.487
This is CanLab, a workstation for reverse engineering a CAN
bus.

2
00:00:04.487 --> 00:00:06.590
We start by loading a capture.

3
00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:13.040
This one is ten seconds of traffic in SavvyCAN format, about
six and a half thousand frames.

4
00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:15.213
The FRAMES tab is the raw view:

5
00:00:15.213 --> 00:00:22.014
every frame in time order, with its timestamp, arbitration ID,
bus, length, the eight data bytes,

6
00:00:22.014 --> 00:00:25.519
and the gap since the last frame with the same ID.

7
00:00:25.519 --> 00:00:31.899
A byte lights up green when it changed, so you can see at a
glance which parts of a message

8
00:00:31.899 --> 00:00:33.792
carry something that moves.

9
00:00:33.792 --> 00:00:38.518
On the left, every arbitration ID the capture contains,
grouped by bus,

10
00:00:38.518 --> 00:00:42.246
with how often it appears and how many frames there are.

11
00:00:42.246 --> 00:00:45.241
Ten IDs here, from one hertz up to a hundred.

12
00:00:45.241 --> 00:00:48.902
Selecting an ID opens it in the inspector on the right:

13
00:00:48.902 --> 00:00:54.760
the last ten frames in hex, a per-byte activity bar showing
which bytes actually change,

14
00:00:54.760 --> 00:00:57.689
and minimum, maximum and mean for each byte.

15
00:00:57.689 --> 00:01:02.016
That is usually the first question you ask of an unknown
message.

16
00:01:02.016 --> 00:01:05.377
The SIGNALS tab classifies every message for you.

17
00:01:05.377 --> 00:01:09.836
It runs offline, with no API key and nothing leaving the
machine.

18
00:01:09.836 --> 00:01:15.118
For each ID it reports the frame count, the rate, the entropy
of the payload,

19
00:01:15.118 --> 00:01:16.558
and a suspected role:

20
00:01:16.558 --> 00:01:22.732
a sensor carrying a physical value, a status message, a
counter, or a diagnostic response.

21
00:01:22.732 --> 00:01:27.534
These are heuristics that point you at the interesting
messages first,

22
00:01:27.534 --> 00:01:30.072
not identifications to trust blindly.

23
00:01:30.072 --> 00:01:31.895
AUTO-RE does the tedious part.

24
00:01:31.895 --> 00:01:35.662
It sweeps every message at once, looking for rolling counters,

25
00:01:35.662 --> 00:01:39.611
bytes that increment each frame and wrap, and for checksum
bytes,

26
00:01:39.611 --> 00:01:44.958
by testing whether a byte is the sum, the exclusive or, or the
nibble sum of the others.

27
00:01:44.958 --> 00:01:49.636
A match only counts if it beats simply guessing the byte's
most common value,

28
00:01:49.636 --> 00:01:55.287
so padding does not read as a checksum, and a relation that
holds for every byte of a message

29
00:01:55.287 --> 00:01:59.297
is thrown away rather than reported, because it localises
nothing.

30
00:01:59.297 --> 00:02:04.948
Counters and checksums are not signals, and knowing which
bytes they occupy removes them from

31
00:02:04.948 --> 00:02:05.616
the search.

32
00:02:05.616 --> 00:02:07.769
That sweep tells you which byte.

33
00:02:07.769 --> 00:02:14.093
To find out which algorithm, the CHECKSUM GUESSER takes one
message and one byte and tries all

34
00:02:14.093 --> 00:02:14.564
twelve:

35
00:02:14.564 --> 00:02:21.091
plain and inverted sums, two's complement, nibble sums, CRC-8
in its SAE J1850 and AUTOSAR forms,

36
00:02:21.091 --> 00:02:25.935
and the manufacturer variants used by Hyundai, Toyota, Honda
and Subaru.

37
00:02:25.935 --> 00:02:31.250
It fits on the first seventy per cent of the capture and
validates on the rest,

38
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:37.575
and reports both numbers, so an algorithm that merely
memorised the training frames is visible

39
00:02:37.575 --> 00:02:41.208
as a high training score with a poor validation score.

40
00:02:41.208 --> 00:02:47.139
The same tab measures per-bit entropy across a message to
suggest where one signal ends and the

41
00:02:47.139 --> 00:02:47.889
next begins.

42
00:02:47.889 --> 00:02:50.074
Bits that never change are padding;

43
00:02:50.074 --> 00:02:53.508
bits that change constantly belong together in a value.

44
00:02:53.508 --> 00:02:58.128
It is a suggestion for the boundaries, which you then confirm
by decoding.
